NAME
       journald.conf - settings of the systemd journal

SYNOPSIS
       /etc/systemd/journald.conf
       /etc/systemd/journald.conf.d/*.conf

DESCRIPTION
       systemd-journald collects log messages from the kernel, from services
       and from programs, and stores them in the journal, which journalctl
       reads. Its settings are in the [Journal] section of
       /etc/systemd/journald.conf (drop-in files in journald.conf.d override
       them). The shipped file has every setting commented out, showing the
       default. After a change run systemctl restart systemd-journald.

SETTINGS
       Storage=auto|persistent|volatile|none
              Where the journal is kept.
              volatile: only in memory, under /run/log/journal; lost at
              every reboot.
              persistent: on disk, under /var/log/journal (created if
              needed); kept across reboots.
              auto (the default): on disk if /var/log/journal exists,
              otherwise in memory. On RHEL 9 that directory does not exist
              at first, so the journal is lost at reboot until you change
              this.
              none: keep nothing.

       Compress=yes|no
              Compress large entries.

       SystemMaxUse=SIZE, SystemKeepFree=SIZE
              Limit the disk space the persistent journal may use, or keep
              this much free (for example 500M). By default 10% (max 4G) and
              15% of the file system.

       RuntimeMaxUse=SIZE
              The same for the journal in /run.

       MaxRetentionSec=TIME
              Remove entries older than this (such as 1month).

       ForwardToSyslog=yes|no
              Pass messages on to a syslog socket. The default is no: on
              RHEL 9 rsyslog reads the journal itself (its imjournal
              module) and writes /var/log/messages and the other text logs.

EXAMPLES
       Keep the journal across reboots:

           mkdir -p /etc/systemd/journald.conf.d
           printf '[Journal]\nStorage=persistent\n' > /etc/systemd/journald.conf.d/00-keep.conf
           systemctl restart systemd-journald
           ls /var/log/journal

       Or edit the main file: remove the # in front of Storage= and set it:

           sed -i 's/^#Storage=.*/Storage=persistent/' /etc/systemd/journald.conf
           systemctl restart systemd-journald

       After a reboot, see earlier boots:

           journalctl --list-boots
           journalctl -b -1

FILES
       /run/log/journal/
              The journal in memory.

       /var/log/journal/
              The persistent journal.

SEE ALSO
       journalctl(1), systemd-journald(8), rsyslogd(8)
